  "semantic_claims": [
    {
      "claim_id": "majority-ground",
      "proposition": "The majority decides the Article III question rather than resting on 8 U.S.C. 1188(g)(2), and holds that the Department of Labor's in-house adjudication of civil penalties and back wages against an H-2A employer falls outside the public-rights exception: applying SEC v. Jarkesy and Tull, it treats the claim as legal in nature (penalties and back pay for breach of work-contract terms), and it confines Oceanic Steam's immigration exception to the admission, exclusion and removal of aliens and exactions tied to them, rejecting the government's 'condition on a government-granted privilege' theory as a basis for agency adjudication."
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "ground-breadth",
      "proposition": "The majority's ground is confined to the immigration public-rights exception as applied to post-entry workplace terms-and-conditions enforcement: it does not announce that every civil penalty must be adjudicated in an Article III court, does not overrule Atlas Roofing or Oceanic Steam, and expressly reserves agency penalty adjudications closer to immigration status (such as employer-sanctions penalties under 8 U.S.C. 1324a) and those in the tax, customs and public-benefits settings."
    }
  ],
